From: oci-hermit <noreply@github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] hw/i386: TSC counts nanoseconds of virtual time

Under TCG the guest TSC followed the host's cycle counter: rdtsc on x86-64
hosts, nanoseconds on others. The guest kernel then had to calibrate it
against the PIT, which is slow under emulation and fails on loaded hosts;
microvm has no HPET or ACPI PM timer to fall back on, so the guest was left
without a clock and hung at boot.

Make the TSC count nanoseconds of QEMU_CLOCK_VIRTUAL, a 1 GHz TSC on every
host that stops with the VM, and tell the guest its frequency on the kernel
command line (tsc_early_khz=1000000), so it never calibrates. oci-hermit
only runs TCG, so the KVM, HVF and WHPX paths, which do not use this
function for the guest TSC, are unaffected.

SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
---
--- a/hw/i386/x86-cpu.c
+++ b/hw/i386/x86-cpu.c
@@ -23,6 +23,7 @@
 #include "qemu/osdep.h"
 #include "system/whpx.h"
 #include "system/cpu-timers.h"
+#include "qemu/timer.h"
 #include "trace.h"
 
 #include "hw/i386/x86.h"
@@ -34,7 +35,12 @@
 /* TSC handling */
 uint64_t cpu_get_tsc(CPUX86State *env)
 {
-    return cpus_get_elapsed_ticks();
+    /*
+     * Nanoseconds of virtual time: a 1 GHz TSC on every host, which the
+     * guest is told about (tsc_early_khz=1000000) instead of calibrating it
+     * against the PIT, which is slow and unreliable under TCG.
+     */
+    return qemu_clock_get_ns(QEMU_CLOCK_VIRTUAL);
 }
 
 /* IRQ handling */
